    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "Looking back at your life, do you remember struggling with this same kind of difficulty staying focused when you were a child or in school?",
      "targeted_symptom": "additional_requirement_1",
      "patient_response": "Yeah, I do. Actually, it goes way back. Even in grade school, I remember... well, my parents used to go to those parent-teacher conferences. They always held them in November, right around Thanksgiving. My mom would always bake these incredible pumpkin pies for the school bake sale they'd run at the same time. She used actual pumpkins, not the canned stuff. It takes forever to roast them down and puree it, but the taste is just completely different. I tried making it her way once, maybe ten years ago, but I burned the crust. My oven runs hot, I think. Or maybe the seal on the door is going bad. \n\nBut anyway, they'd come back from those conferences, and it was always the same story. The teachers would say I was a bright kid, but I was always daydreaming. I'd be looking out the window instead of at the chalkboard. And my homework... half the time I'd do it and just leave it sitting inside my desk. Those old wooden desks with the lift-up tops, you know? They were great for hiding things. I kept a whole collection of baseball cards in mine. But yeah, the teachers were always having to tap on my desk and remind me to finish my classwork. So I guess it's been happening since before I was even twelve.",
